<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philippines</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Philippines is an archipelago in South-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, colonised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> from the sixteenth century and named after Philip II. It remained one of Spain's last colonies after the American independence wars, until it was lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1898. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it was occupied by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a>, leaving lasting legacies of resentment.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philippines.jpg" alt="Philippines" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Philippines.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Brunei's power in the <b>Philippines</b> was challenged by Spain, whose forces under Miguel López de Legazpi took Manila from its Muslim rulers, relatives and allies of Brunei, in 1570–1571.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The kilns of Gò Sành near Vijaya made olive-green and brown glazed pottery, at its peak in the 14th to 16th centuries and found as far away as Egypt, the Emirates, Malaysia and the <b>Philippines</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/champa">Champa · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>It joined the <b>Philippines</b> as one of only two predominantly Catholic states in Asia and is the only sovereign country in Asia with Portuguese as an official language.</blockquote><a href="/empire/east-timor">East Timor · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The destruction wrought across China, Korea, the <b>Philippines</b>, and Southeast Asia left resentments that affected regional geopolitics for decades.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It spread the Castilian language, Catholicism and Spanish institutions across most of the Americas and to the <b>Philippines</b>, and its silver drove trade from Europe to China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…spread to Jambi, Kampar, Bengkalis, Siak, Aru and the Karimun Islands, through the peninsula, to Java and the <b>Philippines</b>; the rulers of Kampar and Indragiri converted through Muzaffar Shah's influence, and two of Java's Wali Sanga, Sunan Bonang and Sunan Kalijaga, are said to have studied in Malacca.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
